leading up to the release of Elden ring  I had fallen out of love with video  games I had played games my entire life  and I think like most people the older I  got the harder it was to really dive  into the world of a game like I could  when I was a kid becoming an adult  sometimes requires you to put your  imagination to the side in order to live  and work in reality for a lot of people  video games are a complete escape from  that reality and whether or not that's  truly healthy it allows people to  connect some of my greatest  relationships have come from those I've  met in video game communities I truly  value my time playing these games with  people I otherwise would not have ever  met and that's a really special thing  for me I fell out of love with gaming at  the start of the pandemic with the  lockdown I obviously started playing  games a little bit too much and relied  on it more than I ever did before and  with the releases of new games that I  was excited for but honestly just fell  short that were blatantly unfair  finished with a full price tag still  attached to it I found myself angry with  the gaming industry as a whole when  eldering was announced I was hyped up  just like everybody else but I was also  terrified I really did not want to see  from software release an unfinished game  and we just have another hyped up  release disappoint us yet again this  game was gonna be it for me if a  developer that I looked up to as the  creator of some of the best games ever  made would also fall into the greedy  fire pit that the gaming industry is  seemingly turning into today I told  myself that I would be done I would no  longer spend my time in money playing  video games but Elden Ring released and  I knew the second I walked out into Lim  grave that I was about to play something  really special then I looked out at this  beautiful view of lorenia lakes and I  knew that this game was going to be  something different then I got to the  capital city and my jaw literally  dropped to the floor then I found redone  and the lead up to his boss fight and  knew that this might have been the best  moment I've personally ever had in  gaming then I find out that getting to  the archery isn't the end of the game  but there's even more to explore to the  North and then even more to explore to  the North and then I found this vast  underground area with many different  branching levels and bosses to discover  I'm sure you're getting my point here  the game just kept going you ever wish  you could just erase your memory and  play a game all over again well I had  this feeling four or five different  times when playing Elden ring I knew the  game was going to be big but I never  thought that a game could be this big  and constantly over and over again  throughout this massive World make me  stop in my tracks put down the  controller and just stare at the screen  I have never played a game that made my  jaw drop from the spectacle I was  looking at more than Elden ring the game  has problems every game does whether  it's balancing of weapons or a steep  increase in the in-game game difficulty  whatever it may be I don't think anyone  can make the claim that Elden ring isn't  absolutely beautiful all of these areas  are stunning and filled with intense  detail that creates a matching style to  basically everything in the game I've  heard some people say that the game just  doesn't look realistic and other games  have better graphics I see Elden ring in  all of its areas as beautiful works of  art like paintings it doesn't need to  look perfectly realistic realistic  Graphics don't make a beautiful game and  it definitely doesn't make a good game  eldenraine clearly went with a style  that matches the gameplay and the story  itself everything in the game descends  my imagination wild with possibilities  of how these areas came to be what  happened here and what could be over the  next Hill the gameplay requires the  player to be curious to look deeper into  the meanings of everything that we can  see the game is so mad massive and full  of interesting content that it could  take us years to connect all of its  themes and to truly understand  everything it's the same feeling as when  you're looking at a painting there could  be many different interpretations of a  painting and I believe what makes a  great painting is for it to have the  viewer of it use their imagination to  try and understand it the meaning of a  painting for one person may be  completely different to another person  that's what Eldon ring does for me it  makes me use my imagination to try and  understand the deeper meaning of  everything that I'm looking at that  feeling holds true in every area of this  game the art style gameplay and even the  story is like a Sandbox what you create  from what you're given is really up to  you and while there is a clear story  that we can follow the choices we make  while we journey through this work of  art is up to us and creates a different  meaning for every player burning the ERD  tree down and becoming the law word of  chaos may be really exciting for one  player and completely terrifying for  another leaving the lands between behind  with Ronnie could have a meaning of  weakness and honestly simp-like behavior  for certain players while other players  may see it as the only true path forward  even the main ending of just becoming  the new Elden Lord may be the only thing  players ever wanted throughout their  entire Journey while for others they  paid attention to the corruption  throughout the world and would do  anything to avoid becoming the Elden  Lord but all of this depends on your  playthrough your journey throughout the  game as a player and who you are as a  person molds the game for you no other  game has made me feel this way no other  game has given me that childlike Wonder  of immersion within my imagination like  Eldon ring has I've made many videos  about this game since release and if you  haven't noticed yet I like to theorize  on the deeper meanings of the game more  so than the actual facts presented in  the game the facts are important so we  can understand what really happened  within the game's story but just like in  life the facts presented may have  different meanings depending on who's  looking at it and that's what I really  like to dive into Elden ring may not be  the best game ever made in fact it's not  even my favorite Souls game that for me  goes to bloodborne but I think it's safe  to say that for more reasons than just  the graphics Elden ring is the most  beautiful game I've ever played and  honestly thank you to from software for  creating this world and not falling into  the gaming industry Norms that we're  seeing more and more of if there was  going to be a time for from software to  fail it was with Elden ring but I  believe they definitely met and exceeded  all expectations and for myself it  revitalized my love for gaming it gave  me hope that there are developers out  there who aren't just looking for a  quick cash grab and are okay with  releasing a fully priced unfinished  product to their player base or some  remake of a game we played a decade ago  the other part of this is that the bar  has now been raised for other developers  by a lot Elden ring was extremely  successful more so than even from  software predicted I think gamers in  general played eldenring and realized  that they were given something they  haven't seen in a while a Finnish game  with a lot of effort and love put into  its design it showed other developers  that if you just create a good game that  it will sell they don't need to follow  the industry Norms they just need to  create something they care about and put  enough time into to actually finish and  I'm not saying I'm gonna play Alden ring  for the rest of my life it's definitely  not a perfect game but just like a  painting it doesn't have to be perfect  for me to go back look at it from time  to time and enjoy the beautiful world  that it presents and I can just dive  into its world use my imagination and  just have fun like I used to when I was  a kid thank you for watching and let me  know how you feel about the game I'm  planning on creating a a lore series on  many different areas of the game and the  deeper meanings of items landmarks and  characters that we can find in each of  those areas let me know which area you'd  like to see first make sure to subscribe  as we're getting really close to 100 000  subscribers that would be an absolute  dream come true for me and go follow my  twitch where I'm gonna start learning  the speed run for Eldon ring it should  be a good time with all that said I'll  see you in the next video take care
 

